The 2022 video game _Stray_ features rich video detail and a complex 
soundscape. Many segments of the game allow the player to explore a vast urban 
environment textured by visuals of decay and futuristic synth music. These 
gameplay segments are abandoned of life, aside from the cat player-character 
and the unsettling _zerk_ infestation. In the populated parts of the city, the 
player is greeted by a population of robots, called _companions_, who have 
taken the role of humanity as the cities primary residents following a past 
extinction event. These segments of gameplay feature various kinds of diegetic 
music when interacting with the robot citizens who engage in human-like 
activities, despite being created to nothing more than serve living humans. 
This diegetic music in _Stray_ suggests the existence of an emerging musical 
culture which mirrors the human culture that came before it while highlighting 
the robotic qualities of the companions who directly interact with it. This 
musical culture has elements of musical appreciation, performance, 
participatory dance, and composition, all of which serve the characterization 
of companions as both human and robotic.

Our analysis focuses on the 2022 game _Stray_ developed by BlueTwelve Studio 
and published by Annapurna Interactive. While our analysis was limited to the 
PlayStation 4 and PC versions, no notable difference due to localization or 
platform appear to impact the in-game audio experience. Additionally, we seek 
to analyze only the _diegetic_ music of _Stray_. Diegetic sound refers to 
"sound that exists in the fictional space of the game and is heard by 
characters within that space" @vmo. As a rule of thumb, we will consider a 
given sound effect as diegetic music if it is feasible that an in-game 
character could hear the sound and consider it a musical. Sounds are not 
considered diegetic music if there is no clear in-game source of the sound or 
if the sound is created due to an environmental interaction, such as a 
footstep.
